1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,250
It's three in the morning, in an emergency room

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:06,482
A doctor is meeting a woman with cancer for the first time

3
00:00:06,482 --> 00:00:09,839
And nobody in that room knows what she wants

4
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:11,839
So her family has to have the hardest

5
00:00:11,839 --> 00:00:15,296
conversation of their lives, all over again

6
00:00:16,009 --> 00:00:19,018
In India, ninety-eight percent of people with advanced

7
00:00:19,018 --> 00:00:22,250
cancer don't get the palliative care they need

8
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:24,571
Yes, you heard that right

9
00:00:24,571 --> 00:00:26,089
Ninety-eight

10
00:00:26,089 --> 00:00:28,036
That's why we built Sahara

11
00:00:28,036 --> 00:00:29,661
It's an app for people with cancer

12
00:00:29,661 --> 00:00:32,464
and for the families who look after them

13
00:00:32,464 --> 00:00:35,214
We're five people who met at Health-a-thon

14
00:00:35,214 --> 00:00:39,482
Basith and Awaiz run a software company called WeDevIT

15
00:00:39,482 --> 00:00:43,482
Dr Sujay is from IISc, Bengaluru

16
00:00:43,482 --> 00:00:46,625
Dr Sharada is from AIIMS, Mangalagiri

17
00:00:46,625 --> 00:00:49,339
And Shirin is a final-year medical student

18
00:00:49,339 --> 00:00:52,250
Back in August, we had our first team meeting

19
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:55,911
And Dr Sujay told us something that stayed with all of us

20
00:00:55,911 --> 00:00:58,643
Patients do tell you what they want at the end of life

21
00:00:58,643 --> 00:01:00,411
But nobody writes it down

22
00:01:00,411 --> 00:01:04,018
And in an emergency, nobody can find it

23
00:01:04,018 --> 00:01:06,089
So we did what technical people do

24
00:01:06,089 --> 00:01:07,554
We built everything

25
00:01:07,554 --> 00:01:10,482
Screen after screen, all for doctors

26
00:01:10,482 --> 00:01:13,232
Then we asked ourselves one question

27
00:01:13,232 --> 00:01:15,321
Why would a family install all this?

28
00:01:15,321 --> 00:01:19,207
So we pivoted, towards the patient and the family

29
00:01:19,813 --> 00:01:24,232
And we kept one thing at the centre: the goals of care talk

30
00:01:24,232 --> 00:01:27,393
That's when the doctor, the patient and the family sit

31
00:01:27,393 --> 00:01:31,161
down together, and decide what kind of care she wants

32
00:01:31,161 --> 00:01:35,107
With everyone's consent, the doctor records that talk

33
00:01:35,107 --> 00:01:37,393
AI writes out who said what

34
00:01:37,393 --> 00:01:40,804
For Indian languages, we use Sarvam AI

35
00:01:40,804 --> 00:01:44,268
Then the AI checks that nothing important was missed

36
00:01:44,268 --> 00:01:45,750
It goes through thirteen topics

37
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:48,286
taken from India's end-of-life care guidelines

38
00:01:48,286 --> 00:01:49,946
written by the national critical

39
00:01:49,946 --> 00:01:52,671
care and palliative care societies

40
00:01:53,295 --> 00:01:55,268
Then the AI turns the conversation

41
00:01:55,268 --> 00:01:57,768
into a goals of care document

42
00:01:57,768 --> 00:02:00,143
That's a clinical record of her wishes

43
00:02:00,143 --> 00:02:01,786
Does she want CPR?

44
00:02:01,786 --> 00:02:02,929
A ventilator?

45
00:02:02,929 --> 00:02:04,286
Intensive care?

46
00:02:04,286 --> 00:02:06,946
And where does she want to be cared for?

47
00:02:06,946 --> 00:02:08,714
Nothing is final until the doctor

48
00:02:08,714 --> 00:02:11,929
reads it, corrects it, and signs it

49
00:02:11,929 --> 00:02:15,207
Then the family signs too, and it's locked

50
00:02:15,759 --> 00:02:17,536
We also took legal advice on the

51
00:02:17,536 --> 00:02:20,000
wording and the format of that document

52
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,779
But goals of care is only the start

53
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:26,768
Sahara stays with the family, every single day

54
00:02:26,768 --> 00:02:28,643
Patients track their symptoms

55
00:02:28,643 --> 00:02:31,232
They rate how they're feeling on a distress thermometer

56
00:02:31,232 --> 00:02:34,161
and they mark where it hurts on a body map

57
00:02:34,161 --> 00:02:37,107
Their doctor can see how it's changing over time

58
00:02:37,107 --> 00:02:38,464
There's a mood journal, for the days

59
00:02:38,464 --> 00:02:40,429
that are hard to put into words

60
00:02:40,429 --> 00:02:44,196
They take a photo of a prescription, or a medicine strip

61
00:02:44,196 --> 00:02:45,339
AI works out the medicines

62
00:02:45,339 --> 00:02:48,179
and reminds them about every dose

63
00:02:48,179 --> 00:02:49,357
They upload their reports

64
00:02:49,357 --> 00:02:52,554
and ask a question in plain words

65
00:02:52,554 --> 00:02:54,929
AI answers, and shows the exact

66
00:02:54,929 --> 00:02:56,946
line in the report it came from

67
00:02:56,946 --> 00:03:00,457
Their next hospital visit is scheduled right in the app

68
00:03:01,045 --> 00:03:02,429
Family members get their own login

69
00:03:02,429 --> 00:03:05,071
so everyone stays in the loop

70
00:03:05,071 --> 00:03:07,571
There's an emergency SOS button

71
00:03:07,571 --> 00:03:10,125
And a QR card that carries the patient's

72
00:03:10,125 --> 00:03:13,000
wishes, contacts and medicines

73
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,893
We also scraped public listings

74
00:03:14,893 --> 00:03:16,536
and put over sixteen hundred

75
00:03:16,536 --> 00:03:20,679
palliative care centres, from across India, on one map

76
00:03:20,679 --> 00:03:23,750
It even shows where to find oral morphine

77
00:03:23,750 --> 00:03:25,143
And there's a community

78
00:03:25,143 --> 00:03:27,982
so no family goes through this alone

79
00:03:27,982 --> 00:03:30,446
Families don't talk in just one language

80
00:03:30,446 --> 00:03:34,589
So Sahara understands English, Hindi, Telugu

81
00:03:34,589 --> 00:03:38,696
Urdu and Kannada, even when they're mixed in one sentence

82
00:03:38,696 --> 00:03:40,857
Now, back to that emergency room

83
00:03:40,857 --> 00:03:42,875
One scan of her QR card, and the

84
00:03:42,875 --> 00:03:45,554
doctor knows exactly what she wanted

85
00:03:45,554 --> 00:03:49,232
Oh, wait. Late one night, something hit us

86
00:03:49,232 --> 00:03:51,571
We kept asking how the patient feels

87
00:03:51,571 --> 00:03:53,232
Nobody was asking her daughter

88
00:03:53,232 --> 00:03:55,187
So caregivers get their own wellbeing

89
00:03:55,187 --> 00:03:58,464
check too, and someone to call

90
00:03:58,464 --> 00:04:00,732
All of this took three months

91
00:04:00,732 --> 00:04:03,125
We had a lot of meetings with our doctors

92
00:04:03,125 --> 00:04:05,161
We met our mentor for a review

93
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:08,232
And we ran a survey of doctors and hospitals

94
00:04:08,232 --> 00:04:12,125
We saved our work to GitHub almost six hundred times

95
00:04:12,125 --> 00:04:14,482
Each of those is called a commit

96
00:04:14,482 --> 00:04:16,661
It's one more improvement to the app

97
00:04:16,661 --> 00:04:19,107
It's built with free and open-source tools

98
00:04:19,107 --> 00:04:21,482
and it runs on a cloud server

99
00:04:21,482 --> 00:04:23,143
When Sahara got shortlisted

100
00:04:23,143 --> 00:04:25,875
we rebuilt it as a full Android app

101
00:04:25,875 --> 00:04:29,214
And it's live on Google Play today

102
00:04:29,214 --> 00:04:33,118
Sahara. Care that follows what matters to the patient

